context:
In the free electron (Drude) model for a metal, the conduction electrons are approximated as a gas of free electrons. The key parameters are n, the electron number density, and τ, the mean time between collisions (relaxation time). Let p denote electron momentum, k the electron wave vector, v the electron velocity, F the force on an electron, E the applied electric field, j the current density, e the magnitude of the electron charge, m the electron mass, ħ the reduced Planck constant, σ the electrical conductivity, and ρ the electrical resistivity. Show that in this model the conductivity can be written as σ = n e^2 τ / m. Then estimate the collision time for electrons in copper, assuming one conduction electron per atom. Use the given data and constants below, and report the final numerical estimate as τ_Cu.

Given data and constants:

$$
\rho = 1.7\times 10^{-6} \unit{\Omega cm}
$$

$$
\rho = 1.7\times 10^{-8} \unit{\Omega m}
$$

$$
 n = 8.5\times 10^{22} \unit{cm^{-3}}
$$

$$
 n = 8.5\times 10^{28} \unit{m^{-3}}
$$

$$
 e = 1.6\times 10^{-19} \unit{C}
$$

$$
 m = 9.11\times 10^{-31} \unit{kg}
$$

The Drude-Lorentz formula for the complex dielectric constant of a solid is $$
\varepsilon(\omega)=1+\frac{\omega_p^2}{(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)-i\omega\tau^{-1}}
$$ Here \omega_p is the plasma frequency, \omega_0 is the interband transition frequency (energy gap), and \tau is the electron scattering time. We use e for the electron charge magnitude, m for the electron mass, \epsilon_0 for the vacuum permittivity, \hbar for the reduced Planck constant, n for the conduction-electron number density, J for the electric current density, E for the electric field, P for the polarization, D for the electric displacement, \sigma(\omega) for the complex conductivity, \chi for the electric susceptibility, N_A for Avogadro's number, \rho for the mass density, M for the molar mass, and i for the imaginary unit. For plotting in part (a) we introduce the photon-energy variable U defined by U=\hbar\omega and express U in \unit{eV}. We also use \lambda_0 to denote a characteristic optical wavelength associated with the reddish color of Cu, used to estimate \omega_0

subquestions.subproblem:
At room temperature a reasonable value for Cu is $$
\tau=10^{-14}\unit{s}
$$ Give order-of-magnitude estimates of \omega_p and \omega_0 for this metal. You may make use of the characteristic color of Cu to determine \omega_0. Plot the real and imaginary parts of \varepsilon(\omega) as a function of \omega, expressed via the photon energy U=\hbar\omega in \unit{eV}.

subquestions.solution:
Assume each Cu atom contributes one free electron. The conduction-electron number density is $$
n=\frac{N_A\rho}{M}
$$ Using $$
N_A=6.02\times 10^{23}\unit{mol^{-1}}
$$ $$
\rho=8.9\unit{g/cm^3}
$$ $$
M=63.5\unit{g/mol}
$$ we obtain $$
n=8.4\times 10^{22}\unit{cm^{-3}}
$$ $$
n=8.4\times 10^{28}\unit{m^{-3}}
$$ For the electron, $$
\frac{e^2}{\epsilon_0 m}=3\times 10^{3}\unit{m^2 s^{-2}}
$$ The plasma frequency is $$
\omega_p=\sqrt{\frac{n e^2}{\epsilon_0 m}}
$$ Numerically, $$
\omega_p=1.6\times 10^{16}\unit{s^{-1}}
$$ The corresponding photon energy is $$
\hbar=6.6\times 10^{-16}\unit{eV s}
$$ $$
\hbar\omega_p=6.6\times 10^{-16}\unit{eV s}\times 1.6\times 10^{16}\unit{s^{-1}}
$$ $$
\hbar\omega_p=10.5\unit{eV}
$$ $$
\hbar\omega_p\approx 10\unit{eV}
$$ From the reddish color of Cu, take $$
\lambda_0\approx 6\times 10^{-7}\unit{m}
$$ which gives $$
\omega_0\approx 3\times 10^{15}\unit{s^{-1}}
$$ and $$
\hbar\omega_0\approx 2\unit{eV}
$$ At room temperature, $$
\tau=10^{-14}\unit{s}
$$ so $$
\hbar\tau^{-1}=6.6\times 10^{-2}\unit{eV}
$$ $$
\hbar\tau^{-1}\approx 10^{-1}\unit{eV}
$$ Define the real and imaginary parts by \varepsilon_R(\omega) and \varepsilon_I(\omega). Then $$
\varepsilon_R(\omega)=1+\frac{\omega_p^2(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)}{(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)^2+\omega^2\tau^{-2}}
$$ $$
\varepsilon_I(\omega)=\frac{\omega_p^2\omega\tau^{-1}}{(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)^2+\omega^2\tau^{-2}}
$$ For plotting as a function of photon energy U in \unit{eV}, define $$
U=\hbar\omega
$$ Using \hbar\omega_p\approx 10\unit{eV}, \hbar\omega_0\approx 2\unit{eV}, and \hbar\tau^{-1}\approx 10^{-1}\unit{eV}, the approximate forms are $$
\varepsilon_R(U)\approx 1+\frac{10^{2}(4-U^{2})}{(4-U^{2})^{2}+U^{2}10^{-2}}
$$ $$
\varepsilon_I(U)\approx \frac{20}{(4-U^{2})^{2}+U^{2}10^{-2}}
$$ These are plotted in Fig. 1.15.
